<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pyrrhus of Epirus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">319–272 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pyrrhus was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molossians">Molossians</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, sheltered as an infant by the Taulantian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glaukias">Glaukias</a>. Invited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Taras</a> in 280 BCE, he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> at Heraclea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascoli-piceno">Asculum</a> at such cost that a 'Pyrrhic victory' became proverbial. Checked at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Beneventum</a> in 275 BCE, he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> in 272 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyrrhus-of-epirus.jpg" alt="Pyrrhus of Epirus" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pyrrhus_MAN_Napoli_Inv6150_n03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pyrrhus was the son of the Epirote prince Aeacides and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phthia">Phthia</a>, a Thessalian noblewoman. Aeacides was a cousin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympias</a>, which made Pyrrhus a second cousin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. Aeacides supported Olympias against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> and was dethroned when his soldiers mutinied. Pyrrhus's family fled north to Glaucias of the Taulantians, an Illyrian tribe, and Pyrrhus was raised by Glaucias's wife Beroea, a Molossian of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeacidae">Aeacid dynasty</a>. Glaucias refused to give him up to Cassander. He then served his brother-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-i-of-macedon">Demetrius Poliorcetes</a>, fought at Ipsus in 302 BCE, and in 298 BCE was taken hostage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> under a treaty between Demetrius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I Soter</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In Alexandria Pyrrhus married Antigone, Ptolemy's stepdaughter. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> died in 297 BCE, Ptolemy gave him men and money and sent him back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>. He ruled jointly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoptolemus">Neoptolemus</a>, then had him murdered at a dinner after learning of a plot against his life. He co-ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> after driving out Demetrius, and was himself driven out in 284 BCE. Tarentum asked him to lead its war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and he arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 280 BCE with 20,000 infantry, 3,000 cavalry and 20 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>. His costly victories at Heraclea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascoli-piceno">Asculum</a> gave rise to the phrase Pyrrhic victory.</p><p>In 278 BCE the Greek cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> asked him to drive out <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, and he lifted the siege of Syracuse and was proclaimed king of Sicily. His assaults on Lilybaeum failed, and compulsory contributions and garrisons angered the Sicilians. In 275 BCE the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manius-curius-dentatus">Manius Curius Dentatus</a> repulsed his attack near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Beneventum</a>, where he lost half of his elephants. He gave up the campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, keeping only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Tarentum</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Pyrrhus seized the Macedonian throne from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-ii-gonatas">Antigonus II Gonatas</a> in 274 BCE, but made himself unpopular by letting his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> plunder the royal tombs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aigai">Aegae</a>. In 272 BCE the Spartan Cleonymus asked him to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>. The assault was thwarted, and Pyrrhus was killed in a street battle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a>. Plutarch records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> ranked him first among commanders, or in another passage second only to Alexander. The phrase Pyrrhic victory comes from his reported remark after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascoli-piceno">Asculum</a> that another such victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> would ruin him utterly.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pyrrhus of Epirus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Demetrius, called Poliorcetes, ‘the Besieger’, kept his fleet and several Greek cities, and in 294 BCE had the young Alexander V murdered and made himself king of Macedon, only to be driven out by <b>Pyrrhus of Epirus</b> and Lysimachus in 288 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pyrrhus of Epirus</b> won costly victories at Heraclea in 280 BCE and Asculum in 279 BCE but withdrew after Beneventum in 275 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Romans most likely took a full interest in these Greek myths after the war against <b>Pyrrhus of Epirus</b> in 280 BC.</blockquote><a href="/work/aeneid">Aeneid · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pyrrhus</b> of the Kingdom of Epirus made it his capital and adorned it with palaces and statues.</blockquote><a href="/state/ambracia">Ambracia</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pyrrhus</b> of the Kingdom of Epirus died fighting in its streets in 272 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/argos">Argos</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Hellenistic period Corcyra was held by Agathocles of Syracuse and <b>Pyrrhus</b> of the Kingdom of Epirus.</blockquote><a href="/state/corcyra">Corcyra</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Epirus. Died in Argos, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.63,22.72&amp;year=-289" aria-label="Argos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5117 810 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Pyrrhus of Epirus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5567 1080h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5567 1080h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=37.63,22.72&amp;year=-289">What was here in 289 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…one other such would utterly undo him.&#8221;</p><footer>Plutarch, Life of Pyrrhus &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; his reply, as Plutarch reports it, to one who congratulated him on his victory over the Romans &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…it is impossible for any man to avoid his fate; but neither he nor any other Italian shall have much satisfaction in engaging with me.&#8221;</p><footer>Plutarch, Life of Pyrrhus &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; to an officer warning him of an Italian horseman, at Heraclea &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…we will live at our ease, my dear friend, and drink all day, and divert ourselves with pleasant conversation.&#8221;</p><footer>Plutarch, Life of Pyrrhus &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; to his adviser Cineas, who asked what he would do once he had conquered the world &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
